set('nineteen:4','nil represents absence; only nil and false are falsy, while 0, "" and [] are truthy.','An empty method returns nil, but a nonempty method implicitly returns its last evaluated expression even without an explicit return keyword. nil remains a normal assignable object, and a false Boolean can be a valid result.','def answer; 42; end\np answer # 42\np !!0 # true',['Use nil? when absence must be distinguished from false.'],['Source’s statement about methods without explicit return often returning nil needed correction.']);
set('nineteen:16','Common String operations include upcase, downcase, capitalize, strip, split, gsub, include? and length.','Choose substitution scope deliberately: sub replaces the first match, gsub every match. String methods normally count characters rather than bytes where appropriate, but a user-perceived grapheme can contain several code points. Bang/non-bang naming communicates risk by convention.','p "hello".gsub("l", "L") # "heLLo"\np " hELLO ".strip.capitalize # "Hello"',['The original gsub output comment is incorrect.'],['Corrected "heLLo" to "heLLo" with both lowercase l matches replaced; exact result is heLLo.']);
// Above comment wording is normalized for the human report.
get('nineteen:16').issues=['Corrected source gsub output: replacing every l in hello yields heLLo (two uppercase Ls), not heLLo with only one replacement.'];
set('nineteen:17','Many Ruby operators dispatch methods, such as a + b becoming a.+(b), but syntax such as assignment and short-circuit &&/|| cannot be redefined as ordinary operator methods.','Overload only operators with meaningful domain semantics. Defining + for a vector lets addition remain readable while preserving its contract. Operator precedence remains fixed by Ruby’s grammar.','class Vector\n  attr_reader :x, :y\n  def initialize(x, y)\n    @x, @y = x, y\n  end\n  def +(other) = Vector.new(x + other.x, y + other.y)\nend\np (Vector.new(1, 2) + Vector.new(3, 4)).x # 4',['The source’s blanket “Yes” needs the syntax exceptions.'],['Qualified claim that operators are methods and replaced questionable compact multiple-assignment initializer with standard syntax.']);
set('nineteen:19','Use # for line comments and =begin/=end at line starts for block comments. Conventionally methods and locals use snake_case, classes/modules use CamelCase and value constants use ALL_CAPS.','? and ! suffixes express intent rather than enforce return values or mutation in the language. Predicates conventionally return truthy/falsy results; bang names usually mark a more dangerous counterpart, which can mean mutation, exception raising or another semantic risk.','name = "ruby"\np name.upcase # "RUBY"; name is unchanged\nname.upcase!\np name # "RUBY"',['A non-bang method such as String#concat can still mutate.']);
set('nineteen:28','<=> returns a negative, zero or positive number according to ordering, or nil for incomparable operands; Comparable derives other comparison methods from it.','The common built-in result values are -1, 0 and 1, but the contract is the sign. Include Comparable and implement a consistent <=>. Return nil for unsupported comparisons rather than assuming every other object has the expected fields.','class Version\n  include Comparable\n  attr_reader :number\n  def initialize(number) = @number = number\n  def <=>(other)\n    return nil unless other.is_a?(Version)\n    number <=> other.number\n  end\nend',['Comparable does not automatically supply a Hash equality/hash contract.']);
set('nineteen:29','Enumerable provides methods such as map, select, reduce, sort, group_by and min to objects that implement each.','An Enumerable implementation must yield its elements consistently; comparison-dependent methods also need usable comparisons. Returning an Enumerator when each has no block makes custom collections easier to chain.','class Playlist\n  include Enumerable\n  def initialize(tracks) = @tracks = tracks\n  def each(&block)\n    return enum_for(:each) unless block\n    @tracks.each(&block)\n  end\nend\np Playlist.new(["a", "b"]).map(&:upcase)',['The iteration protocol is the foundation; some derived operations need additional semantics.']);
set('nineteen:31','Know fetch, dig, merge, each_with_object, transform_values, group_by and default-value patterns for Hash-based work.','fetch distinguishes a missing key from a stored nil or false and may raise KeyError or use a default/block. dig walks nested supported containers and can raise TypeError for an incompatible intermediate object. Hash.new(0) works for numeric counting, but shared mutable defaults such as Hash.new([]) are a common bug.','groups = Hash.new { |h, key| h[key] = [] }\ngroups[:ruby] << "Asha"\np groups # {:ruby=>["Asha"]}',['group_by is an Enumerable operation that returns a Hash, not only a method about an existing Hash.']);
set('nineteen:35','Keyword parameters name arguments explicitly and may be required or defaulted. In Ruby 3, positional Hash arguments are separated from declared keyword parameters.','Use **kwargs to receive/forward keyword pairs and *args for positional arguments. A method taking one positional options hash can still receive keyword-looking syntax as that hash when it declares no keyword parameters. For wrappers use ... or explicit forwarding according to the interface.','def create_user(name:, role: "member")\n  { name: name, role: role }\nend\np create_user(**{ name: "Asha" })',['Do not claim all final hashes and keywords are interchangeable.']);
set('nineteen:36','Struct creates lightweight mutable record classes; Data.define (Ruby 3.2+) creates value-like classes without member writers.','Use Data when fixed attributes communicate a value object, and Struct when field reassignment is intended. Data objects do not recursively freeze member values, so a stored Array or String can still change. “Preferred” depends on the application interface, not a universal rule.','Event = Data.define(:tags)\nevent = Event.new(tags: ["ruby"])\nevent.tags << "rails" # nested Array remains mutable',['Reference: https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/3.4/Data.html'],['Qualified “immutable” as shallow member protection; preserved nested mutability.']);
set('nineteen:40','||= caches a truthy result but recomputes when the stored value is nil or false.','For a valid falsy result, distinguish “not computed” with defined?, instance_variable_defined? or a sentinel. Shared-object memoization additionally needs synchronization or an immutable initialization strategy; the operator alone is not a thread-safety mechanism.','def enabled?\n  return @enabled if instance_variable_defined?(:@enabled)\n  @enabled = calculate_enabled\nend',['Per-request caching and long-lived shared caches have different concurrency and lifetime concerns.']);
set('nineteen:44','In ordinary CRuby threads, the Global VM Lock limits simultaneous execution of Ruby code, while blocking I/O can release it. Threads are useful for I/O concurrency; CPU parallelism may require processes, suitable Ractors or another runtime.','JRuby and TruffleRuby have different threading implementations, and native extensions may release the lock for computation. Fibers provide cooperative scheduling, optionally through a scheduler, but do not themselves imply CPU parallelism. Pick the runtime and model from measured workload and library support, and do not treat the GVL as protection for application-level invariants.','require "thread"\nqueue = Queue.new\nworkers = 2.times.map { Thread.new { queue << :done } }\nworkers.each(&:join)\n2.times { p queue.pop }',['Reference: https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/3.4/Thread.html']);
set('nineteen:45','Ruby values have classes, and class objects are instances of Class. Class inherits Module, then Object, with BasicObject at the root; ordinary object-specific methods live in singleton classes.','Class methods are singleton methods on class objects. The source’s 5.singleton_class example raises TypeError in CRuby rather than returning a hidden eigenclass: Integer, Float and Symbol do not expose arbitrary singleton classes. nil, true and false have special singleton_class behavior.','p String.class # Class\np Class.superclass # Module\np Object.superclass # BasicObject\np Object.new.singleton_class',['Reference: https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/3.4/Object.html#method-i-singleton_class'],['Corrected invalid 5.singleton_class example and “each object has its own singleton class” overstatement.']);
set('nineteen:47','Refinements provide lexical method changes activated with using, limiting the reach of a patch compared with reopening a global core class.','Define refine inside a module, then activate it in an allowed lexical scope. Activation affects code defined in that scope rather than being a dynamic switch on every call stack. This can avoid global collisions, but lookup nuances and library interoperability should be understood.','module Shouting\n  refine String do\n    def shout = upcase + "!"\n  end\nend\nusing Shouting\np "hi".shout # "HI!"',['Reference: https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/3.4/syntax/refinements_rdoc.html']);
set('nineteen:48','instance_eval runs with an object as self; class_eval/module_eval target a class/module context; instance_exec also supplies arguments to the block.','These support small DSLs and dynamic method definitions. Block forms retain lexical constant lookup, while string evaluation has additional parsing/context behavior and is unsafe with untrusted text. instance_eval does not mean that every def inside it adds ordinary instance methods to the object’s class.','object = Object.new\nobject.instance_exec(3) { |n| @count = n }\np object.instance_variable_get(:@count) # 3',['Prefer a normal explicit API when changing evaluation context makes the code harder to understand.']);
set('nineteen:50','A Ruby process can retain memory indefinitely through reachable objects or leak native memory even though garbage collection exists.','Compare RSS, allocation rates and live-object counts over time; use GC.stat, ObjectSpace snapshots and appropriate sampling or allocation profilers. An unbounded cache or retained request object is an application leak, while allocator fragmentation can keep RSS high without the same live-object growth. Bound lifetime or ownership, verify the fix, and treat restarts as mitigation.','p GC.stat.slice(:heap_live_slots, :total_allocated_objects)',['Allocator changes and worker restarts may mitigate symptoms but do not prove the retention bug is fixed.']);
set('nineteen:51','Profile allocation-heavy paths, remove avoidable intermediates, reuse values safely and use lazy pipelines when only part of a source is consumed.','Avoid unsafe shared mutable reuse and do not assume symbols always win. Frozen literals can reduce repeated allocations where semantics permit. A lazy producer plus lazy map/select avoids materializing unneeded arrays; eagerly selecting over an infinite source never finishes.','p (1..Float::INFINITY).lazy.select(&:even?).first(3) # [2, 4, 6]',['Measure retained memory and CPU as well as allocation counts.']);
set('nineteen:56','Avoid shared mutable state where practical; otherwise protect the complete invariant with synchronization or use a suitable thread-safe queue/data structure.','The GVL does not make multi-operation business logic atomic and is not a portable synchronization guarantee across Ruby runtimes. A Mutex protects related state only when every access follows the locking policy. Database races additionally require database constraints, locks or atomic statements; Ruby-level synchronization does not coordinate separate worker processes.','mutex = Mutex.new\ncount = 0\nthreads = 4.times.map { Thread.new { 100.times { mutex.synchronize { count += 1 } } } }\nthreads.each(&:join)\np count # 400',['Do not hold locks around slow external calls without considering contention and deadlocks.']);
set('nineteen:57','case/in matches and deconstructs structured data, binding matched variables; case/when mainly tests pattern === target.','Pattern matching arrived experimentally in Ruby 2.7 and evolved in Ruby 3.x. Array/hash patterns, guards, pinning and deconstruct/deconstruct_keys can express protocol-aware matching. An unmatched case/in without else can raise NoMatchingPatternError, so define the failure path deliberately.','case { role: "admin", permissions: ["read", "write"] }\nin { role: "admin", permissions: [*, "write"] }\n  puts "can write"\nelse\n  puts "other"\nend',['Version-check newer pattern syntax rather than treating all of it as present in every Ruby 3 release.'],['Clarified experimental introduction in 2.7 and missing unmatched-pattern behavior.']);
set('nineteen:59','If a.eql?(b) is true, a.hash must equal b.hash. Hash collisions are allowed, but equal keys must agree on their hash.','Define a stable equivalence relation and compute the hash from the same fields with compatible comparison semantics. The source compares coordinates with ==, which makes numeric values such as 1 and 1.0 compare equal while their component hash/eql? behavior can differ. Use eql? consistently or normalize values. Keys must not mutate in a way that changes their hash while stored.','class Coord\n  attr_reader :x, :y\n  def initialize(x, y)\n    @x, @y = x, y\n  end\n  def eql?(other)\n    other.instance_of?(self.class) && x.eql?(other.x) && y.eql?(other.y)\n  end\n  def hash = [self.class, x, y].hash\nend',['Hash lookup involves hash buckets and then eql? matching.'],['Corrected potential eql?/hash contract mismatch in source’s numeric-coordinate example.']);
set('thirteen:concept-5','Singleton methods are per-object behavior; Rails attribute methods use metaprogramming, but ordinary Active Record attributes are not generally implemented by hand-defining singleton methods on every retrieved record.','Rails generates attribute methods in model-associated modules and uses missing-method hooks as needed to trigger generation. The source’s per-user singleton definitions can illustrate Ruby syntax but are not a faithful description of Active Record’s implementation. Distinguish a teaching analogy from actual framework internals.','user = Object.new\ndef user.label = "one object"\np user.label',['Reference: https://api.rubyonrails.org/classes/ActiveRecord/AttributeMethods.html'],['Corrected source’s misleading claim about Rails generating per-instance singleton attribute methods.']);
set('thirteen:concept-9','nil? asks whether the receiver is nil; empty? asks a supported container whether it has no elements.','An empty Array is not nil, and nil does not implement empty?. nil? does not safely test an arbitrary local that was never defined: evaluating an unknown local/method can raise first. Rails blank? also treats whitespace-only strings and false as blank, with separate Active Support semantics.','p [].nil? # false\np [].empty? # true\np nil.nil? # true',['An empty string is still truthy in an if condition.']);
set('thirteen:concept-13','and/or and &&/|| short-circuit but have different precedence, especially relative to assignment.','Use parentheses where call/assignment meaning might be ambiguous. Do not use or as a file-error handler around File.open: failed open normally raises, and Ruby has no standard die method. Rescue an expected filesystem exception instead.','result = nil or true\np result # nil\nresult = nil || true\np result # true',['Style preferences about and/or usage are recommendations rather than language restrictions.'],['Corrected nonworking File.open(... ) or die example.']);
set('thirteen:concept-18','Idiomatic Ruby favors clear Enumerable operations, consistent method-call syntax and explicit distinction between text keys and identifier keys.','Style guidelines are conventions, not correctness proofs. The source changes {"one"=>1} to {one:1}, which changes String keys into Symbol keys and is not a semantics-preserving rewrite. Ternaries and modifier conditions are appropriate only when readable. Do not add a gap between a called method name and parenthesized arguments.','p({ "one" => 1 }["one"]) # 1\np({ one: 1 }["one"]) # nil',['Choose a project style guide, but preserve behavior when applying it.'],['Corrected supposedly equivalent String-key-to-Symbol-key style transformation.']);
// More targeted source-question answers without a recoverable full prompt.
set('thirteen:replay-Validate string against dictionary','The title alone does not define what “validate” means. If the intended variant is word break, dynamic programming can determine whether a string can be segmented into dictionary words.','A standard variant marks positions reachable by valid dictionary tokens. dp[0]=true; for each end position, accept a prefix if some earlier reachable position is followed by a dictionary word. This is an illustration, not an answer to the unspecified original replay problem.','def word_break?(text, words)\n  dictionary = words.to_h { |word| [word, true] }\n  reachable = Array.new(text.length + 1, false)\n  reachable[0] = true\n  1.upto(text.length) do |finish|\n    reachable[finish] = (0...finish).any? { |start| reachable[start] && dictionary.key?(text[start...finish]) }\n  end\n  reachable.last\nend',['O(n²) candidate splits, with substring allocation/hash costs beyond constant-time lookup; exact constraints are missing.']);
set('thirteen:replay-Minimum tree depth','For the standard binary-tree minimum-leaf-depth variant, breadth-first search returns the depth of the first leaf.','Count depth as one for the root in this illustration and zero for an empty tree. A node with just one child is not a leaf, so min(left_depth,right_depth)+1 needs special treatment for absent children. This is not the missing original problem statement.','def minimum_depth(root)\n  return 0 unless root\n  queue = [[root, 1]]\n  index = 0\n  while index < queue.length\n    node, depth = queue[index]\n    index += 1\n    return depth unless node.left || node.right\n    queue << [node.left, depth + 1] if node.left\n    queue << [node.right, depth + 1] if node.right\n  end\nend',['O(n) time and O(n) queue storage for this straightforward implementation.']);
set('thirteen:replay-Move Stones Over Grid','The title does not specify the grid, allowed moves, start counts or target condition, so a correct unique solution cannot be recovered.','Request the missing constraints. For one common variant, pairing surplus stones with empty cells using Manhattan-distance assignment works when moves are orthogonal and no obstacles exist; with obstacles, weighted edges or different targets, both costs and the optimization algorithm change. Do not invent those assumptions as source facts.','Example clarification: Are moves between adjacent cells only? Must every cell end with one stone? What is the grid size? Are obstacles present?',['A title is insufficient evidence for claiming a runnable solution.']);
// Editorial and preparation FAQs are kept rather than silently discarded.
const prep=[
 ['Focus on Ruby’s object model, truthiness, collections, methods, blocks/procs/lambdas, modules, exceptions and practical Rails/database behavior.','Practice each concept with a small executable example and a follow-up edge case. Interview frequency depends on the role; no list guarantees coverage.'],
 ['Practice timed small coding problems while explaining assumptions, invariants, complexity and edge cases aloud.','Implement a correct baseline, verify it with examples, then optimize a measured bottleneck. Review actual Ruby return values and mutation behavior instead of only memorizing algorithm names.'],
 ['Prioritize objects, local versus instance state, Enumerable, block semantics, exception handling and safe database/web interactions.','For a Rails role add routing, Active Record associations and SQL, authorization, jobs, tests and production debugging. Use the job description to set depth.'],
 ['Solve varied problems, trace your code manually, test failure cases and compare alternative implementations after finishing.','Keep a mistake log and revisit problems with a changed constraint. Explain time and space costs in terms of actual Ruby operations, including substring/array allocations.'],
 ['Advanced preparation includes lookup order, singleton classes, metaprogramming, GC/allocation behavior, concurrency and production performance.','Connect internals to real debugging or design decisions rather than reciting implementation trivia. Ruby runtime and Rails version affect available tools.']
];
